          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022:\u0022pages/concepts/retrieval-patterns.md\u0022,\u0022content\u0022:\u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Retrieval Patterns\\ndescription: Techniques for combining and ranking results from multiple retrieval methods, including hybrid retrieval that merges sparse and dense search.\\ntimestamp: 2026-07-14T00:00:00Z\\ntags:\\n  - concept/Retrieval\\n  - concept/Ranking\\n  - pattern/RAG\\nconfidence: medium\\nconfidence_reason: \\u0022One authoritative source on hybrid retrieval as production pattern; existing page not yet corroborated.\\u0022\\n---\\n\\n## Hybrid Retrieval\\n\\nHybrid retrieval combines sparse lexical search (e.g. BM25) with dense vector search, then merges the two ranked result sets to capture both exact term matches and semantic similarity. This pattern has become the default retrieval approach in production RAG systems.\\n\\n### Ranking Fusion\\n\\nA common technique for combining results from multiple retrieval methods is **reciprocal rank fusion**, which merges ranked result sets without requiring scores to be normalized across different retrieval algorithms. This approach is robust and handles heterogeneous ranking scores from different search backends.\\n\\n### Use Cases\\n\\nHybrid retrieval consistently outperforms either sparse or dense search alone on queries that mix:\\n- Rare identifiers (error codes, product SKUs)\\n- Natural language intent and semantic meaning\\n\\nThis makes it particularly valuable for production RAG systems that need to handle diverse query types.\\n\u0022}"
